WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.010
- Zach from "The Try Guys" has come out

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.260
and said that he has an
autoimmune condition.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.130
I'm about to watch the
video and react to it.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.210
Let's get to it.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.043
Be whoop!

00:00:08.043 --> 00:00:10.920
- I have had really bad back issues,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.510
basically my entire life.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.470
- Perfect, I'm an osteopathic physician.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:15.303
This is gonna go really well.

00:00:15.303 --> 00:00:17.930
- I was going to the
chiropractor when I was 10.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.030
I had to have like an ergonomic
backpack in middle school.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.030
- I mean, everyone should have

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:23.610
an ergonomic backpack in school.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.190
In fact, my theory is that
a lot of the scoliosis

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.720
that kids are developing these days

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.520
come from uneven backpacks,
wearing them on one shoulder,

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.010
or even those desks where you
can only lean on one side.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.000
- I ache, I get stiff.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.120
It kind of started before
I went to BuzzFeed.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.950
A lot of my time would
be spent at a computer.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.740
And I noticed I would just...

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.840
When I sneezed, I could feel
the pain shoot from my back

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.900
through the front, through my chest cage.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.440
- The way that he's describing his pain,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.050
right away tells me that there's
some kind of inflammation

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.950
happening at the rib joints.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.290
Because remember the ribs attach
to the front of your chest,

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.020
the sternum, at least the upper ribs here.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.860
And then also to your spine in
the back, the thoracic spine.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.513
- I have a bit of a hypochondria.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.967
And so, whenever something's
wrong with me, I'm like,

00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:06.880
"Cancer, it's cancer, cancer."

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.600
So I told my doctor about this pain

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.700
and he says, "That's not
a thing, you're good."

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.370
- If you're a doctor and
a patient's coming in

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.390
with a high level of
anxiety about a condition,

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.450
and you know they're susceptible
to health related anxiety,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.110
you have to right away
be ultra transparent

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.410
and honest about what you're
doing and why you're doing it.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:25.420
Not just give statements like,
"You're good," and move on.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.530
- I decided it was time
to go to the chiropractor.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:28.363
We took an x-ray.

00:01:28.363 --> 00:01:30.870
Turns out I have (beep) scoliosis.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.720
- So scoliosis doesn't
always require treatment.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.440
We blame a lot of problems
on patient scoliosis

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.440
when it's actually not the main cause.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.000
It's very easy in a patient
who's having back pain,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.430
caused by something completely
different, to get an x-ray

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:44.750
and blame it all on the scoliosis.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.330
In fact, it's the lazy thing to do.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.000
A small degree of scoliosis

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.140
shouldn't be causing
significant discomfort.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.500
In fact, most cases of
scoliosis that we see

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.160
are extremely mild and are asymptomatic.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.820
- Now, for anyone who has scoliosis,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:57.920
they'll be the first people to tell you

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.080
that my scoliosis is minor.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.850
It's not the kind that
you would get surgery for.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.010
I tried a lot of things to help this.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:05.200
I changed my diet.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.520
- I don't know what the diet
would help with scoliosis with,

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.990
unless he had a bone
mineral deficiency there.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.460
The main thing that you do
for mild scoliosis like this

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.860
is not even bracing,
not surgery, obviously.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:15.930
Physical therapy.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.450
Doing some exercise, some
lifestyle modifications,

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:19.760
to make sure you're not wearing a book bag

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.640
on only on one side.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.187
- The pain built to a point
where I couldn't sleep.

00:02:24.187 --> 00:02:28.320
And I woke up at like 4:45 in pain.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.150
This was every night for months.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:36.700
Laying down, hurt, being in my bed hurt.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.300
I mean, living hurt.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.133
I'm talking about,

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.750
I would wake up in the middle
of the night five times.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.010
And all during this, I'm
working a full-time job,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.330
but when you're not sleeping,
it eats away at you mentally.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.470
- It absolutely does because
you don't recover well,

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.610
you're constantly in a
hypersympathetic state,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.820
where it's like you're basically
being chased by a lion,

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.770
you're never relaxing,
you're never digesting,

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.160
you're never repairing and healing.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.470
And, as a doctor, you
have to ask these things.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.600
Because when we ask how much disability

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.110
a certain injury is causing you,

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.560
it's not about whether or
not you can walk or run,

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.710
or lift or not.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.900
It's also about, can you rest adequately?

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:12.880
Can you sleep well?

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:13.713
Can you sit well?

00:03:13.713 --> 00:03:15.620
Can you perform the
duties at your job well?

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.404
- I start screaming, I think,

00:03:17.404 --> 00:03:19.310
and I feel bad for the doctor,

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.560
but I was like, "Hey, this can't continue.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.080
I'm losing my mind."

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.720
I got referred to a rheumatologist.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.207
And, within minutes, the
rheumatologist is like,

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:30.040
"Oh, yeah, I think I know
what's wrong with you."

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:30.873
And I'm like, "What?"

00:03:30.873 --> 00:03:33.130
- I'm interested what
conversation they had

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.040
in the medical office

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.200
that led him to go to
see a rheumatologist.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.610
Who's essentially an autoimmune
joint disease expert.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:40.890
Why they went that route

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.300
versus a spinal surgeon, for example.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.590
It could have been that
he wasn't interested

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:45.720
in spine surgery,

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.700
but there has to have been tip offs

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.835
to some sort of autoimmune process.

00:03:49.835 --> 00:03:52.540
- I had to go in for an
MRI, which was scary.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.300
'Cause at that point, I didn't
know what was wrong with me.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.740
He just referred me to an MRI

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.360
and I'm like, "Oh, God, what is this?"

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.230
- MRI is magnetic resonance imaging

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.980
where you have giant magnets
essentially spin around you

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.870
to give us really good quality images.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:04.703
It's really loud.

00:04:04.703 --> 00:04:05.800
It's very claustrophobic.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.900
Some of my patients actually
need benzodiazepine medications

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.040
which are anti-anxiety medications

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.920
to even be able to get an MRI.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.000
They do a great job at
showing soft tissue.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.150
Like an x-ray, for example,

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:18.320
will show bone quite well or joints well,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.160
the MRI would show us cartilage,
tendons, ligaments, organs.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.190
- It turns out I have
ankylosing spondylitis.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.120
- Ankylosing spondylitis is basically

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.130
an inflammatory autoimmune condition

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.650
where it usually starts
in the sacroiliac joint,

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.690
which is the joint between your hips

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.370
and one of the lower portions
of your spine, your sacrum.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.310
Aching get so bad that it starts spreading

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:42.090
to other vertebral joints
moving up your back

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.480
where you develop stiffness,

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:44.910
and in the worst conditions,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.180
actually the joints fuse,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.400
and you become very stiff.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.390
It becomes very painful.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:51.020
Think about inflammation in the joint.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.870
- This was about four months ago.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:56.420
I am happy to report that since that time,

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.920
I've been on medication,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.400
and I have felt better than I have felt

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.060
since, like, childhood.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.210
I take a drug called HUMIRA.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:07.420
So, no matter where I am,
no matter what I'm doing,

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.250
I have to stop what I'm doing,

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.150
and give myself a little shot.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:13.110
- You ready to get poked?
(Zach chuckles)

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.240
- We hold on to Zach's medicine.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.510
- I have to take this
medication twice a month,

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:18.343
the same time.

00:05:18.343 --> 00:05:21.340
So it's 8:40 am and I
gotta take this shot.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.180
- This is a medication that's used

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:27.050
as a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.940
And these DMARDs work
through different processes

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.870
but the medication he takes
targets tumor necrosis factor

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.560
and it actually allows the
decrease of inflammation

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.335
where he has an unnecessary increase.

00:05:38.335 --> 00:05:39.168
- And so what's great

00:05:39.168 --> 00:05:40.945
is that you never actually see the needle.

00:05:40.945 --> 00:05:42.110
- Oh.
- Right.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:42.950
- That's nice.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:44.110
- See this window right here?

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.420
Some of the liquid's gonna pop out of it.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:47.253
(device clicks)

00:05:47.253 --> 00:05:50.140
- Whoa.
- Ow, ow, ow.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:51.540
But that's it, I'm done.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.210
So I started my medication

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:57.310
and for a few months, life was incredible.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:58.860
I was able to sleep throughout the night

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.110
without waking up in pain.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.210
I wasn't stiff throughout the day.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:03.043
- It's important to remember,

00:06:03.043 --> 00:06:05.440
as he's talking about his
experience with this medication,

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.510
that is not a cure for his condition,

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.720
it's a disease-modifying drug.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.760
So it changes the course, the development,

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.250
making the progression slower.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.630
That's the most important takeaway.

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:16.270
- During this time I was instructed

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:17.680
to keep up with physical activity.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.240
No, no, Bowie!

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:24.240
But, as the months passed,
I got a little lax.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:25.890
I was back to where I was.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:29.840
The truth is, I started
ignoring my disease

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.440
the second I could.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.430
The moment that my
medicine started working,

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.340
I stopped paying attention to it.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:40.020
So it was time to go back to the doctor,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:43.080
finally ready to accept
and confront my symptoms,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.560
and ready to attack this sickness head on.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:46.513
And,

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.053
he said I was fine.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.080
My inflammatory markers were still low.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.280
- If a patient is coming in

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.630
and saying they're suffering
again with their illness,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.920
that they feel like it's making a return,

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:01.710
they're going back to where they were,

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:02.617
and I come in and I say,

00:07:02.617 --> 00:07:05.870
"No, your inflammatory cells are good,"

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.630
that's not adequate medical care.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:09.410
We never treat lab values.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:11.010
We don't treat inflammatory cells.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.390
If a patient's coming in and
telling me they have back pain,

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:14.270
if a patient's telling me

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.260
that they have terrible crippling anxiety

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:17.530
and depressive symptoms,

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.850
I don't say, "Oh, your labs
look good, so, deuces."

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.670
- I decided to make my
health a full-time job.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.890
Physical therapy twice a week.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:27.410
Yoga and stretching first
thing when I wake up

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.230
and last thing before I go to bed.

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:31.150
Long walks with Bowie every single day,

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.880
instead of letting someone
else walk him for me.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:37.950
And, exercise, especially on
the days that I don't want to.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.680
Accepting that sometimes being
late to work in the morning

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.730
is a necessary component to my health.

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:43.980
- The movement component

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.490
and the healing that comes
from moving is amazing.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.120
Because whether it's a medication,

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.960
a massage, physical therapy,

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.840
all of that is in place
so your body heals itself.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.680
And a body that's not in motion

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.480
will continue staying not in motion,

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.860
and continue being in pain.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.113
- And, after all that,

00:08:01.237 --> 00:08:02.289
I feel okay.

00:08:02.289 --> 00:08:04.000
(Dr. Mike chuckles)

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.040
I still have a baseline of pain most days.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:12.320
I don't know that I'm ever
gonna fully be 100% again,

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.010
but I am done ignoring my pain.

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:15.870
- That's really powerful

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.380
because acceptance is a
big part of chronic pain.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.780
A lot of times we promise patients

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.400
that we can fully get rid of their pain.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:23.820
Go from 100 to zero.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.730
And, a lot of times, that's not feasible.

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:28.730
Most notably so when it
comes to opioid treatments.

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:31.510
Patients that are promised
zero pain are given opioids,

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:32.660
their pain goes to zero,

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:35.850
but then we can't continue
those in many cases, long term,

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.680
unless that there's an endpoint,

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.140
there's a goal for something.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:40.950
Check out my video of Michael Reeves

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.090
and his bladder exploding.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:43.923
What?!

00:08:43.923 --> 00:08:45.680
Click it, it's the most fascinating story.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.410
He's actually in the video with me.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.380
Click here to check that out,

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:50.330
and, as always, stay happy and healthy.

